                  // **********                Feature Engineering                 ***********

Defination: Feature Engineering is the process of Transforming raw data into useful features That helps a machine learing model learn better, It has 4 steps: 

-> Feature Transformation: Changing the form or scale of existing features to make them more suitable for the ML model, here we perfore things like 
            -> Missing Value Updation (Imputation)
            -> Handling Categorical Values using mechanisims like One-Hot-Codint ...etc
            -> Outliers Detection and Handling Them
            -> Feature Scaling 

-> Feature Construction: Creating new features from existing features to give the ML model more useful information.

-> Feature Selection: Choosing the most useful features from the available features and removing irrelevant or unnecessary ones.

-> Feature Extraction: Creating new, useful features from raw or complex data while reducing unnecessary information.




                // **********            Feature Scaling          ***********

Feature Scaling contains 2 types , they are standardization, Normalization
// Standardization
Definition: Standardization (Z-score scaling) is a Feature Scaling technique which puts every column on the same scale by making its mean = 0 and std = 1. 

Why: Columns like age (18-60) and salary (20,000-2,000,000) have very different ranges, so models see the bigger numbers as more important. Standardization gives every column equal footing.

Formula:  z = (x - mean) / std

-> z = 1.5 means the value is 1.5 standard deviations above the mean, no matter what the original units were.

Important points:
-> It does NOT change the shape of the distribution, skewed data stays skewed (only shifts and rescales). To make data normal, use log / power transforms.
-> Outliers still affect it, because mean and std are sensitive to outliers.
-> fit only on train data, transform on both train and test, otherwise it is DATA LEAKAGE.

Code: 
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler

scaler = StandardScaler()
X_train_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)   # learns mean/std from train only
X_test_scaled = scaler.transform(X_test)         # reuses train's mean/std

