// Normalization
Definition: Normalization (Min-Max scaling) is a Feature Scaling technique which squeezes every column into a fixed range, usually 0 to 1.

Formula:  x_scaled = (x - min) / (max - min)

-> min value becomes 0, max value becomes 1, 0.5 means halfway between min and max of that column.

Important points:
-> Outliers affect it even more than standardization, one extreme max/min squashes all other values together (ex: one salary of 10 crore pushes every other value near 0).
-> Output range is fixed (0 to 1), where standardization is not bounded.
-> Same rule: fit only on train data, transform on both train and test, otherwise it is DATA LEAKAGE.

Variants:
-> MinMaxScaler : the standard one above
-> MaxAbsScaler : divides by max absolute value, good for sparse data
-> RobustScaler : uses median and IQR, so it handles outliers well
-> Mean Normalization : rarely used

Standardization vs Normalization:
-> Standardization uses mean and std, output not bounded (about -3 to 3), use when data is roughly bell shaped, or for PCA, linear models
-> Normalization uses min and max, output fixed 0 to 1, use when bounded range needed ex: image pixels (0-255 -> 0-1), neural networks
-> Not sure? start with standardization. Outliers a problem? use RobustScaler.

Code:
from sklearn.preprocessing import MinMaxScaler

scaler = MinMaxScaler()
X_train_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)   # learns min/max from train only
X_test_scaled = scaler.transform(X_test)         # reuses train's min/max

Note: In this video "normalization" means Min-Max, do not mix with Normalizer (scales each ROW to unit length).

Practice: Take a dataset with an outlier in one column, apply both MinMaxScaler and StandardScaler and plot histograms before and after to see the squashing effect.



// **********            Categorical Encoding          ***********

Definition: Encoding converts categorical (text) columns into numbers, because ML models need numerical data.

Flow: Text/Categories -> Encoding -> Numbers -> ML Model

// Ordinal Encoding
-> Used when categories have a meaningful ORDER (Poor -> 0, Average -> 1, Good -> 2).
-> Numbers represent the ORDER.
-> When order matters, pass the order explicitly instead of relying on the auto learned order.
-> Used on input features (X).

Code:
from sklearn.preprocessing import OrdinalEncoder

encoder = OrdinalEncoder(categories=[["Poor", "Average", "Good"]])
X_train_enc = encoder.fit_transform(X_train)   # learns categories from train only
X_test_enc = encoder.transform(X_test)         # reuses train's categories

// Label Encoding
-> Converts categories into numbers, but numbers are just LABELS (Red -> 0, Blue -> 1, Green -> 2).
-> Does NOT represent any order.
-> Commonly used on the target column (y).

Code:
from sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder

encoder = LabelEncoder()
y_enc = encoder.fit_transform(y)


         **************      One-Hot Encoding         **************

-> Used when categories have NO order (Red, Blue, Green).
-> Creates one separate 0/1 column for each category.

        Red  Blue  Green
Red      1    0     0
Blue     0    1     0
Green    0    0     1

Code:
from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder
          
encoder = OneHotEncoder(sparse_output=False)
X_train_enc = encoder.fit_transform(X_train)
X_test_enc = encoder.transform(X_test)

Why One-Hot instead of Label for no-order data:
-> Label Encoding (Red 0, Blue 1, Green 2) makes the model think 2 > 1 > 0, which is a FAKE order.
-> One-Hot avoids this.

Ordinal vs Label:
-> Ordinal = number represents ORDER
-> Label = number represents CATEGORY

Encoding != Scaling:
-> Encoding converts categories into numbers (Poor -> 0, Good -> 1)
-> Scaling changes the range of numbers (100 -> 0.0, 200 -> 1.0)

Rule of thumb:
-> Ordered categories -> Ordinal Encoding
-> No order -> One-Hot Encoding
-> Target / output column -> Label Encoding

Same rule as scaling: fit only on train data, transform on both train and test, otherwise it is DATA LEAKAGE.


// **********            Column Transformer          ***********

Definition: ColumnTransformer lets us apply different steps to different columns in one go.

Why: In real data, some columns are numbers and some are text. Each needs different cleaning. Doing it one by one and joining them back is messy, so we use ColumnTransformer.

Important points:
-> We give it a list of (name, transformer, columns).
-> Columns we don't mention are REMOVED by default. Use remainder="passthrough" to keep them.
-> Output is a NumPy array, so column names are gone. Use get_feature_names_out() to get them back.
-> Same rule: fit only on train data, transform on both train and test, otherwise it is DATA LEAKAGE.

Code:
from sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer
from sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer
from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder

ct = ColumnTransformer(
    transformers=[
        ("impute_age", SimpleImputer(), ["age"]),
        ("encode_city", OneHotEncoder(sparse_output=False), ["city"]),
    ],
    remainder="passthrough"
)

X_train_t = ct.fit_transform(X_train)   # learns from train only
X_test_t = ct.transform(X_test)         # uses what train learned










